Letting $x=-2$, we get the series:

$\begin{align}
\displaystyle\sum\limits_{n=1}^{\infty }\dfrac{n(-2-1)^n}{(n+3)\cdot(-3)^n}
&=\displaystyle\sum\limits_{n=1}^{\infty } \dfrac{n(-3)^n}{(n+3)\cdot(-3)^n}
 \\\\
&=\displaystyle\sum\limits_{n=1}^{\infty } \dfrac{n}{(n+3)}
\end{align}$

By the $n^{\text{th}}$-term test, we know this series diverges.

Letting $x=4$, we get the series:

$\begin{align}
\displaystyle\sum\limits_{n=1}^{\infty }\dfrac{n(4-1)^n}{(n+3)\cdot(-3)^n}
&=\displaystyle\sum\limits_{n=1}^{\infty } \dfrac{n(3)^n}{(n+3)\cdot(-3)^n}
 \\\\
&=\displaystyle\sum\limits_{n=1}^{\infty } \dfrac{(-1)^n\cdot n(-3)^n}{(n+3)\cdot(-3)^n}
 \\\\
&=\displaystyle\sum\limits_{n=1}^{\infty } \dfrac{(-1)^n\cdot n}{(n+3)}
\end{align}$

By the $n^{\text{th}}$-term test, we know this series diverges.
